
Thread-LifeCycle:
------------------------------------------------------------------

-thread goes through different stages from creation to completion:
-🔄 Thread Lifecycle Stages
 There are 5 main states:
 -🟢 1. NEW (Born state):
 Thread t = new Thread();
 👉 Thread is created
 👉 But NOT started yet

-🟡 2. RUNNABLE
 t.start();
 👉 Thread is ready to run
 👉 Waiting for CPU

-🔵 3. RUNNING

 👉 Thread is actually executing:
 run() method
 👉 JVM gives CPU time

-🟠 4. WAITING / BLOCKED

 👉 Thread is paused temporarily
 Reasons:
 sleep()
 wait()
 waiting for resource (like synchronized lock)
 Example:
 Thread.sleep(1000);

 -🔴 5. TERMINATED (Dead)
  👉 Thread finished execution
  run() completed
  👉 Cannot restart ❌
  -NEW → RUNNABLE → RUNNING → WAITING/BLOCKED → RUNNING → TERMINATED
  ---------------------------------------------------
  difference between start() and run():

  start():
  -create new thread
  -call run() internally

  run():
  -just normal method
  -no new thread
-----------------------------------------------------------
we can not restart a thread again
t.start();//OK
t.start();//exception
-----------------------------------------------------------
we use sleep to pause the execution
-----------------------------------------------------------
example:

class MyThread extends Thread {

    public void run() {
        try {
            System.out.println("Thread is running");   // RUNNING stage

            Thread.sleep(1000);  // WAITING stage

            System.out.println("Thread resumed");

        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        MyThread t = new MyThread();  // NEW state
        System.out.println("Thread state: " + t.getState());

        t.start();  // RUNNABLE
        System.out.println("Thread state after start: " + t.getState());
    }
}

